1. Introduction:
The advancement of a reliable photocatalyst with intended band architecture, superior charge carrier segregation, and photo-stability features is a prime requisite for the sake of reducing the impact of our environmental pollution and seek alternative energy production by using sunlight illumination. For years, scientists have persistently investigated different groups of semiconducting type photocatalysts for the aforementioned issues. However, recently the organic–inorganic hybrids widely recognized as metal organic frameworks (MOFs) have gained enormous popularity due to its outstanding advantages such as high surface area, porosity, flexible functionalization, customizable compositions related with unrestricted variations of organic linkers and metal centers, culminating in a diversified spectrum of possible MOF nanostructures [1–7]. As of now, most pristine MOFs demonstrate fast exciton combination; nevertheless, these might lack the proper bandgap, light harvesting tendencies, and water stability, deeming them unsuitable as a robust photocatalyst. Several approaches are being pursued in this direction, such as the insertion of functional groups, foreign moieties, and hetero-junction with different photocatalysts [8,9]. However, most enhanced polymer nanocomposites have some shortcomings, such as; inadequate interaction between respective photocatalysts, undesirable catalytic site obstruction, surface area reduction, poor exciton separation, lower reusability, and so on. Consequently, researchers are exploring for ways to address these constraints of composite photocatalysts nanomaterials leading to a massive necessity for the engineering of single-component type MOF based photocatalysts [10–12]. Typically, it is well known that functionalizing linkers with proper groups can effectively tune the bandgap, thereby combining linkers with different functional groups tends to open up new possibilities for improved bandgap modulation [13–15]. Thus, this mixed linker strategy can be an easy alternative to the complex multistep post synthetic modification paths and hence more detailed studies are imperative to fully contemplate the electronic structures and bandgap modulation in the MOF based photocatalysts consisting of mixed-functionalized linkers [16–18].
Typically addressing the needs for environmental pollutant remediation photocatalysis tends to be a sustainable route in which MOFs play a pivotal role. Amidst various pollutants, antibiotics are widely and persistently found in nature thus making their remediation imperative. Moreover, Tetracycline hydrochloride (TCH) remains the second most recommended antibiotic for animal and human treatments against varied infections [19–21]. Thereby the bulk of TCH passes into the aquatic ecosystem via wastes of living organisms that promotes a pernicious impact on the environment. Additionally the TCH presence in environment triggers the evolution of antibiotic resistant microorganisms leading grave threat to human health. Hence, the eradication of TCH remains a massive concern for the scientific community [22,23]. In this regard, various research groups have utilized MOF based nanomaterials for TCH photo-degradation such as; AgI/BiOI/UiO66 $(90.1\%)$ , $\mathrm{SmVO_{4}/U i O{-}66{-}N H_{2}}$ $(50\%)$ and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@\mathrm{UiO-}66\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ $(75\%)$ [24–26]. In addition, the development of a robust and efficacious energy generation photocatalytic system remains imperative. Since the successful demonstration of water splitting by ‘‘Fujishima and Honda” in 1972 using $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and ultra violet (UV) light researchers have raced to achieve robust photo-catalyst for the green energy generation [27]. In the current era, MOFs with their exceptional characteristics have garnered significant traction towards photocatalytic hydrogen generation [28]. Some of the researchers performed hydrogen evolution by Zr-MOF based nanomaterials such as; UiO-66/CdS $.153.2\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}$ , UiO-66 $\ensuremath{\vert\mathtt{g}\mathrm{-}\ensuremath{\mathsf{C}}_{3}\ensuremath{\mathsf{N}}_{4}}$ $(14.11\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}$ , $\mathrm{g{-}C_{3}N_{4}/U i O{-}66{-}N H_{2}}$ ( $152.2\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1})$ and $g_{-}$ $\mathrm{C_{3}N_{4}/U i O{-}66/N i_{2}P}$ ${\mathrm{200~\upmumol}}$ in $^\textrm{\scriptsize5h}$ ) [29–32]. Most of these are composites of MOF with other nanomaterials and they tend to have certain downsides involving; inadequate interaction between each component, active site blockage, limited exciton separation and feeble reusability leading to their limited performance and usage. Hence, these drawbacks can be overcome by designing a single component MOF based robust visible light responsive photocatalyst.
In this study, we discuss our results for a single-step synthesized mixed linker Zirconium MOF based nano-architecture for photon aided Tetracycline Hydrochloride photo-degradation and Hydrogen production. The pristine Zr-MOFs (UiO-66 and $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ - UiO-66; where, ‘‘UiO” represents ‘‘University of Oslo”) along with its mixed linker composites were comprehensively studied that revealed the composite material shows band gap modulation and enhanced exciton separation complimented with oxygen vacancy that supports its remarkable photocatalytic performance. The band gaps in the mixed linker composite were significantly reduced with respect to UiO-66 (3.68–2.7 eV) and were nearly equal to UiO-66- $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ (2.67 eV). Furthermore, the mixed linker MOF showed enhanced charge separation as well elongated photo-generated electron lifetime leading to superior photocatalytic outputs. Moreover, the mixed linker composite showed high surface area that is essential for providing populous active-sites for reactions. Nevertheless, the aqueous stability as well as reusability tests proves the mixed linker composite to be a stable Zr-framework making worthy for varied photocatalytic applications. Hence, the developed mixed linker Zr-MOF functions as an efficient and promising single-component photocatalyst for TCH degradation and water reduction reaction, whose mechanistic insights have been thoroughly described.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Chemicals used
Benzene dicarboxylic acid (BDC, ${>}98\%$ ), 2- aminobenzenedicarboxylic acid $'B D C\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ , $99\%$ ), nafion-117 solution $\cdot\sim5\%$ in lower aliphatic alcohols/water mixture) and zirconium chloride $\left(\mathsf{Z r C l}_{4}\right.$ , $99.99\%$ ) were procured from Sigma-Aldrich. Reagent-grade ethanol (EtOH, $99\%$ ), methanol (MeOH, $99\%$ , isopropanol $(99\%)$ , N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF, $99.8\%$ ), sodium hydroxide (NaOH, $97\%$ ), hydrochloric acid (HCl, $36.5\substack{-38\%}$ , ferric nitrate $(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}0,$ $98\%$ , sodium sulphate $\mathrm{\bf{Na}}_{2}\mathrm{\bf{S}}0_{4}$ , $99\%$ ) and potassium bromide (KBr, $99.5\%$ ), were purchased from Merck Chemicals and used without any further purification. For all the experimental purposes, deionized water was used.
## 2.2. MOF fabrication
Pristine MOFs (UiO-66 and $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H_{2}}.$ ) were synthesized by following the solvothermal route using our previously reported methods [9,33]. Typically, equimolar $\mathit{'6}\:\mathrm{mmol};$ amount of the metal precursor $\left(\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}\right)$ and linkers (BDC or BDC- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ ) were dissolved in DMF through rigorous stirring for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ using a magnetic stirrer. These solutions of the linker and metal precursor in DMF were added together which was further stirred for $60\ \mathrm{min}$ Then the resulting solution underwent solvothermal treatment for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ at $120^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ in a teflon-lined autoclave followed by cooling at room temperature. Then, the solvothermally treated product was activated in methanol for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ followed by centrifugation at $8000~\mathrm{rpm}$ and overnight drying at $70\ {}^{\circ}{\mathrm{C}}.$ . The dried MOFs were grinded with the aid of an agar mortar pestle and named as UiO$66\substack{-\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}}$ or UiO-66 depending upon the functionalized linkers used. Furthermore, the ditopic mixed linker MOF was prepared under similar conditions as that of the parent MOFs but was modified with a mixed linker strategy as shown in Scheme 1. Herein, different ratios of the two linkers (Linker Ratio BDC:BDC- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ ; $25{:}75/$ UML-1 50:50/UML-2 and 75:25/UML-3 where; UML represents UiO-66 Mixed Linker MOF) are varied at a constant zirconium ion concentration.
## 2.3. Material characterizations
These fabricated MOFs based photocatalysts were thoroughly studied by using various sophisticated characterization techniques. The purity of phase, framework construction and crystalline nature of the fabricated MOFs were evaluated by powder $\mathsf{X}.$ -ray diffraction (PXRD) study which was conducted by Rigaku Ultima-IV X-ray diffractometer $40\up k\up V$ to $40~\mathrm{{mA}}^{\cdot}$ fitted through $\mathsf{C u}\mathrm{K}\mathsf{\ensuremath{\upalpha}}$ source having $\lambda=0.154\mathrm{{nm}}$ , at 2h value of $5^{\circ}-70^{\circ}$ . Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectrometer analysis was done for the functional group interpretation of obtained materials in the range of 4000– $400~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ by using JASCO FTIR-4600-LE with the potassium KBr (potassium bromide) reference. Textural properties were measured by Brunauer–Emmett–Teller or BET surface area analysis method by the $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption–desorption evaluation that was studied by using Quantachrome-NOVA-2200e analyzer. TEM (transmission electron microscopy) was studied for internal morphological features of prepared photocatalysts were carried out by using 200 kV JEOL-JEM-2100, that was associated with energydispersive X-ray (EDS) and elemental mapping analysis. By X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) technique, the oxidation states and chemical composition of the elements of the photocatalyst were studied which is measured through the VG-microtechmultilab-ESCA-3000 spectrometer (Mg-Ka-X-ray nonmonochromatised source). The optical properties of the synthesized samples were performed by using JASCO-V-750 UV–Visible spectrophotometer in the wavelength range of $200{-}800~\mathrm{{nm}}$ using $\mathsf{B a S O}_{4}$ as reference. Using a JASCO FP-8300 fluorescence spectrometer the excitation and emission spectra of photoluminescence (PL) were measured with an excitation wavelength $325~\mathrm{{nm}}$ along with a Xenon lamp as the light source. Time-Resolved Photoluminescence (TRPL) analysis spectra of the fabricated materials were performed by using Edinburgh-FLS920 Fluorescence spectrometer coupled with a multi-channel scaling (MCS) module fitted to the F290H pulsed Xe microsecond flash lamp source. By utilizing IVIUMn-STAT multi-channel analyzer the electrochemical characteristics of the prepared MOFs were characterized with ${\tt N a}_{2}{\tt S O}_{4}$ solution $(0.2~\mathrm{M})$ as electrolyte. The intermediates found by the degradation of TCH during the photocatalysis process were efficiently measured through Liquid Chromatography-Mass Spectrometry (LC-MS) technique, with the TSQ-Quantum Access MAX Triple Quadrupole Mass Spectrometer of Thermo-Fisher Scientific. Furthermore, the evolution of $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ was analysed by Agilent Technology gas-chromatography (GC-7890B).
## 2.4. Linker content determination
## 2.5. Electrochemical investigation
Ahead of the electrochemical tests, the working electrodes of parent MOFs as well as their mixed linker composites were produced by depositing these catalysts over the conducting face of fluorine doped tin oxide (FTO) glasses using the traditional drop casting method. FTO glasses were cleansed ultrasonically in acetone for about $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ , which was repeated many times followed by drying in oven. Slurry was prepared by taking $1~\mathrm{mg}$ photocatalyst which was ultrasonically dispersed in $3~\mathrm{mL}$ of ethanol–water solvent $(1{:}1,\mathsf{V}/\mathsf{V})$ along with $40~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of commercial binder solution (Nafion-117). On the FTO’s conducting face, the dispersed photocatalyst was coated via drop casting technique. These FTOs acted as working electrodes and were electrochemically analyzed by a 3-electrode set up.
## 2.6. Photocatalytic investigation
Fabricated photocatalysts were subjected to photocatalytic TCH degradation tests using UV–visible light under ambient conditions. In a sealed titration flask $50~\mathrm{mL}$ ), $20~\mathrm{mg}$ prepared MOF was mixed with 20 ppm TCH solution $20~\mathrm{mL}$ under a lightless environment for $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ , resulting in proper adsorption of TCH molecules from the bulk of the solution to the MOF surface. Furthermore, the suspension was agitated for $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ in the presence of sunlight using a magnetic stirrer. After the photo-reaction, the suspension was centrifuged and the decant was quantitatively analyzed for extent TCH photo-degradation by using UV–Visible spectrophotometer. Furthermore, the impact of solution pH on photocatalysis was determined by taking pH range of 3–9 for the prepared photocatalysts. The solution pH was regulated by 0.1 M HCl and $0.1\mathrm{~M~NaOH}$ solutions. Additionally the catalyst dosage (UML-2 wt in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of 20 ppm TCH: $10~\mathrm{mg}$ , 15 mg, 20 mg, $25~\mathrm{mg}$ and $30~\mathrm{mg}$ ) and initial TCH concentration ( $20~\mathrm{mg}$ of UML-2 in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ TCH solution having concentration; 10, 20, 30, $40\ \mathrm{ppm};$ on the photocatalytic performance of UML-2 towards TCH degradation was evaluated. The role of coexisting ions on photocatalysis tendency of UML-2 was evaluated using 0.01 M solution of desired ions in presence of $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of 20 ppm TCH. Moreover, terephthalic acid (TA) test and nitroblue tetrazolium (NBT) test were simultaneously carried out to confirm the key role of OH. radicals and $0_{2}\cdot^{-}$ radicals respectively. TA readily reacts with hydroxide radical to form 2-hydroxy terephthalic acid (HTA) whereas NBT instantly reacts with the superoxide radicals in the reaction system. In a characteristic method, $5~\mathrm{mg}$ of TA was mixed with $0.02\ \mathrm{gm}$ of mixed linker MOF (UML-2) and with equimolar amount of sodium hydroxide (NaOH), as TA is not soluble in acidic or neutral medium. While for NBT test, $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of $5\times10^{-5}\mathrm{M}$ solution of Nitrobenzene tetrazolium (NBT) was mixed with $0.02\ \mathrm{gm}$ of UML-2 and then both the solutions are stirred under sunlight for $^{1\mathrm{{h}}}$ . After which both the solutions were filtered separately and analyzed by PL and UV–Vis. Spectrometer respectively. Additionally, the photocatalytic hydrogen production capacities of the prepared MOFs were thoroughly examined by using a quartz batch type reactor (Vol. 0.1 L). Typically in the photoreactor, $_{0.02\mathrm{~g~}}$ of prepared MOF was added to $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of diluted methanol solution $(\mathsf{V}/\mathsf{V};10\%$ MeOH in water) and the Xenon arc lamp $(300~\mathrm{Watt}/\lambda>420~\mathrm{nm})$ at a length of $87~\mathrm{{mm}}$ from the solution was employed as the visible light source for $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ . Reactor contents were stirred vigorously to avoid MOF nanoparticles coagulation also the solution was thoroughly bubbled with $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ gas for $30~\mathrm{min}$ prior to Xe lamp usage. Photo-evolved gaseous products were trapped via simple water displacement method that was subsequently analyzed by Gas Chromatography (GC). Moreover, controlled reactions without catalyst and light source were performed to ascertain their irrefutable role in hydrogen production. All the experiments were performed in triplicate to minimize the experimental errors.
## 3. Result and discussion
## 3.1. Physico-chemical characteristics
Preliminary investigation regarding the crystal structure, formation and phase purity of the parent MOFs along their mixed linker versions were studied through PXRD technique in the $2\theta$ range of $5^{\circ}-60^{\circ}$ as shown in Fig. 1a. Typically, the PXRD result revealed that the parent MOFs; UiO-66 along with UiO- $66\substack{-\mathsf{N H}_{2}}$ were successfully synthesised with aciculate peaks which suggests high crystallinity as well as purity that were indexed and well matched according to the previously reported literatures [33–38]. The mixed linker MOFs showed analogous peaks with that of the pristine MOFs without any additional diffraction peaks suggesting the complete retention of framework integrity and structure with respect to that of the isostructural parent frameworks. A comparative PXRD analysis (Fig. S1) of the physical mixture (1:1) of UiO-66/ UiO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ along with UML-2 suggested that the prepared mixed linker MOFs were not simple mixture of parent MOFs. The broad and less intense peaks were obtained for physical mixture whereas UML-2 showed sharp and intense diffraction peaks that is similar to peaks reported by various research groups for mixed linker type frameworks [13,16,39]. Furthermore, the amino-linker contents in the prepared MOF were evaluated according to Fig. S2. It is revealed that the mixed linker MOFs showed BDC- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ contents of $69.1\%$ , $41.4\%$ and $22.3\%$ for UML-1, 2 and 3 respectively that is quite close to the initially amount of linker taken. Additionally the $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherm approach was used to determine the textural attributes (BET surface area and BarrettJoyner-Halenda (BJH) pore size distribution analysis) of the mixed linker MOF; UML-2. BET adsorption/desorption curve of UML-2 mimics a Type-I isotherm as shown in Fig. 1b, indicating the presence of framework micro-porosity that is akin to the parents; UiO66 and UiO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ [28,33]. The UML-2 had a high surface area about $759\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which is reflected by its superior photocatalytic performance. Despite this, the isotherm pattern of the UML-2 MOF remained quite analogous to that of the pristine MOFs, representing that the framework structure is retained in the one pot mixed linker approaches that is consistent with the results of the PXRD analysis. Further, the pore volume distribution obtained, (Fig. 1b inset) indicates that the fabricated UML-2 is microporous in nature.
Additionally, the FT-IR spectral analysis portrays the presence of characteristics functional groups of parent UiO-66, UiO-66- $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ and their mixed linker UML-1, 2 and 3 samples, as shown in Fig. S3a. Typically for pristine UiO-66 the wide band at $1395~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ portrays the symmetric stretching $\scriptstyle(0=\complement=0)$ of linker carboxylate group although the smaller band at $1510~\mathrm{\cm}^{-1}$ describes the $\mathsf{C}{\mathop{=}}\mathsf{C}$ bond vibration in the benzene ring. Furthermore, some lower frequency vibrational peaks at 520, 660, 742, $810~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were ascribed to Zr-(OC) bond asymmetric stretching, $\scriptstyle0=\mathbf{C}=0$ bending vibrations, $\mathsf{C}{\mathop{=}}\mathsf{C}$ stretching vibration and linker CAH bond vibration respectively. Additionally, in $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ a pair of peaks at 3340 and $3470\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are observed which is associated with symmetrical and asymmetrical stretching vibrational modes of the linker amine group respectively [8,36]. Moreover the $\mathsf{N{\mathrm{-}}H}$ bending and $C{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{N}$ bond stretching vibrational bands are observed around 1640 and $1245~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ respectively, that are associated with the linker’s aromatic amine groups. Additionally, vibration of $\mathsf{C}{\mathop{=}}\mathsf{C}$ linkage of the linker’s benzene ring is expressed by the miniature vibrational modes at $1510~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . However, the vibrational peaks emerging at 487, 667, $760~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are allied for Zr(OC) asymmetric stretching, linker’s carboxylate $\scriptstyle0=\mathbf{C}=0$ bending and $\mathsf{C}{\mathop{=}}\mathsf{C}$ stretching in linker benzene ring respectively [9]. So the FTIR analysis of mixed linker MOFs (UML-1, 2, & 3) showed analogous pattern with slight sifting with respect to the pristine ZrMOFs implying identical chemical bonding environment and functional groups present in their framework. The FT-IR results obtained are comparable to those previously reported results.
X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) analysis was employed for detecting the composition and valence state of various elements in a prepared mixed linker MOF sample. Fig. S3b shows the XPS survey spectrum for UML-2, that supports the presence of Zr, C, O and N that was also verified from elemental mapping and EDS results. Furthermore, as shown in Fig. 2a–d, high resolution XPS (HR-XPS) spectra for each element present in the mixed linker UML-2 MOF were obtained and deconvoluted. The deconvoluted peaks at 284.96, 285.7, and $289.13\mathrm{eV}$ corresponds to the carbon atoms (C1s) implicated in $\mathsf{C}{\mathop{=}}\mathsf{C},$ $C{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{N}\mathsf{H}_{2}$ , and $scriptstyle0=\mathbf{C}-0$ bonding of the linkers respectively. Furthermore, the deconvoluted N1s peaks at 399.81 and 400.65 eV corresponded to the BDC- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ linker’s amine groups, $\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathsf{N}}\ensuremath{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{-NH}_{3}^{+}$ respectively, that are remarkably comparable to the previously reported N1s peaks for pure UiO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ [28]. Additionally, the deconvoluted HR-XPS O1s peaks at 530.15, 531.93, 533.07 eV represents oxygen atoms involving lattice O, Metal-O and oxygen vacancy (OV) respectively [40,41]. Furthermore, the deconvoluted peaks of Zr3d in the prepared UML-2 sample are seen at 183.26 and 185.57 eV that indicates the presence of $\mathrm{Zr~}3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ and $\mathrm{Zr~}3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ tetravalent spin states respectively.[28] To recapitulate, the XPS study demonstrates that a single component mixed linker type UiO-66 series MOF was successfully synthesized. Moreover, the Table S1 summarizes the deconvoluted XPS peak values for UiO-66, UiO- ${\cdot66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}}$ , and UML-2, as well as their related chemical environments, for effortless interpretation.
## 3.2. Morphological studies
Morphological aspects of the one-step fabricated mixed linker MOF UML-2 has been captured through field emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM) and high resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) analysis. In Fig. 3(a and b) the FESEM images of UML-2 reveals a uniform size and smooth surface topology, which is quite similar to that of its parent MOFs; UiO-66 and $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ . Furthermore, the insignificant change in morphology of the mixed linker MOF strongly supports the results seen from PXRD analysis, suggesting analogous framework structure with that of pristine MOFs. Moreover, octahedral morphology of UML2 is distinctly seen from the HRTEM analysis images at 100 and $20\mathrm{nm}$ scales as depicted in Fig. 3c and d respectively. It is noteworthy that octahedral morphology of the mixed linker MOF is well inherited from the pristine MOFs. Additionally, the UML-2 nanocrystals from Fig. 3c is vividly seen to be formed independently and well-dispersed manner that can be reflected through its high surface area value. The hyper-sensitivity towards electron beams of the parent $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs are passed on to the mixed linker MOF which can be easily conferred from the inability to obtain lattice planes through selected area electron diffraction (SAED) analysis as depicted in Fig. 3e. However, the high crystallinity of UML-2 sample is strongly established form the acicular PXRD peaks and its high surface area measurement. Moreover, elemental color mapping (Fig. 3f–i) and EDS (Fig. S4a) analysis outcomes provide further support to the compositional XPS analysis, that shows presence of C, N, O, and Zr in the UML-2 framework. Beside the uniform distribution of elements seen from mapping and EDS, layered image in Fig. S4b suggests that the UML-2 MOF is truly a mixed linker MOF rather than a mixture of individual parent MOF crystals.
## 3.3. Optical and electrochemical characteristics
