$$
Here; A, h, m, a, and $\operatorname{E}_{\mathrm{g}}$ portrays the absorption coefficient, planks constant, incidnet light frequency, proportionality constant and materials optical band gap respectively. Additionally, the parameter $\mathbf{\dot{n}}^{\prime}$ represents the potential electronic transitions corresponding to $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}}=1,3,4\&6$ related to the direct allowed, direct forbidden, indirect allowed and indirect forbidden transitions respectively.[8] In present situation, the designed mixed linker $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF and pristine MOFs displays indirect allowed transition with ${\mathfrak{n}}=2$ . The optical band gap of UiO-66 and UiO- $66\substack{-\mathsf{N H}_{2}}$ was evaluated as 3.6 and $2.67\ \mathrm{eV}$ whereas in fabricated mixed linker $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs series the band gaps were calculated to be 2.68, 2.7 and 2.72 eV for UML-1, UML-2, UML-3 respectively as illustrated in Fig. S5a-e. The mixed linker strategy led to band gap tuning and the reduction in band gap with respect to UiO-66 as well as improving their light absorption capabilities thus enhancing the overall photocatalytic output.[42].
Moreover, photoluminescence (PL) analysis of the fabricated neat and mixed linker MOFs were executed to ascertain the role of mixed linker strategy towards improving the charge carrier generation and dilating the photo-generated exciton separation efficacy, with respect to the pristine MOFs; UiO-66 and UiO- $66\substack{-\mathsf{N H}_{2}}$ as shown in Fig. S5f. Interestingly the photoluminescence peak intensities for mixed linker MOFs showed notable reduction, which suggests an enriched lifetime of the photo-generated excitons as compared with the pristine counterparts. This behavior of mixed linker MOFs may be attributed to the presence of two distinct linkers and oxygen vacancies (OVs) in the framework, that tends to promote long-lived excited states and superior exciton separation capabilities [42–44]. Amidst the synthesised mixed linker MOFs, the UML-2 displays slight enhanced stability of the photogenerated charge carriers correlated to its undermost PL peak intensity that can also be supplemented with their EIS analysis results and maximum photocatalytic performance.
Additionally, the lifetime of photo-excited electrons was also examined using time resolved photoluminescence (TRPL) analysis of the UiO-66, UiO-66- ${\cdot}\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ , and UML-2 composite, as shown in Fig. S6 and Table S2 that is fitted to an equation for a biexponential model as shown in Equation 2.
$$
R(t)=A_{1}\mathrm{exp}\{-t/\tau_{1}\}+A_{2}\mathrm{exp}\{-t/\tau_{2}\}
$$
Where, R is the normalized emission intensity, t is the time after pulsed laser stimulation, A is the amplitude, and $\boldsymbol{\tau}$ is the individual component’s exciton lifespan. The average lifetime $(\tau_{\mathrm{avg}})$ of two exponential decay is indispensable to illustrate the overall TRPL character and can be calculated by Eq. (3).
$$
\tau a\nu g=\frac{A_{1}\tau_{1}^{2}+A_{2}\tau_{2}^{2}}{A_{1}\tau_{1+}A_{2}\tau_{2}}
$$
where $\tau_{1}$ and $\tau_{2}$ are electron trapping time and formation of excitons, respectively. The fluorescence life times were assimilated by fitting the decay profile with two exponential terms. The average excited state lifetime of UiO-66, UiO- $66\substack{-\mathsf{N H}_{2}}$ and UML composite are 471, 504 and 667 ps respectively. The average longer lifetime of composite indicates higher lifetime of photo-generated electrons and further supports the PL experimental outcomes.[40].
Typically, Mott-Schottky (MS) electrochemical technique was exploited towards the evaluation of flat band potential, band edge structure and charge transfer pathways in the mixed linker hybrid MOFs as illustrated in Fig. S7. The flat band potentials obtained through MS analysis remains as a key parameter to determine the prepared MOFs band edge and pathways followed by the photo-generated excitons [45]. The mixed linker UML-2 MOF has flat band potential of $-0.62\mathrm{~\eV}$ against Ag/AgCl electrode, as obtained through the extrapolation of ${\mathsf{C}}^{-2}=0$ plot. Thereby, the LUMO of UML-2 were evaluated using Eq. (4) [46].
$$
