Here, T, E, C, $\Nu_{\mathrm{a}},\Nu_{\mathrm{d}},$ , q and $\mathbf{k}_{\mathrm{b}}$ are the absolute temperature, applied potential, space charge capacitance, accepter density, donor density, electronic charge and Boltzmann’s constant respectively. $\in\mathbf{\Omega}_{0}$ and $\in$ are the vacuum permittivity and dielectric constant of prepared semiconductor nano-material. Furthermore, from the flat band potential, band structure was evaluated by using Eq. (4) with Highest Occupied Molecular Orbital $(\mathrm{HOMO})=2.17$ and Lowest Unoccupied Molecular Orbital $(\mathrm{LUMO})~=~-0.53$ as expressed in Scheme 3. From our earlier reported work the parents MOFs; UiO-66 and UiO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ has band structure of ${\mathrm{HOMO}}=2.9.$ · LUMO $\mathbf{\epsilon}=\mathbf{\epsilon}_{-0.7}$ and $\mathsf{H O M O}\ =\ 2.03$ , LUM $0~=~-0.64$ respectively [28,33].
Furthermore, the electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) tool was used to examine the effective exciton separation and migration in single component mixed linker $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs. A shorter semi-circular arc size is associated with reduced interfacial charge transfer resistance, indicating improved charge transport characteristics in nanoparticles [48,49]. In Fig. S8, the UML-2 version seems to have a reduced semi-circular arc than that of the original UiO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ MOF, indicating an effective decrease in the recombination capabilities of photo-generated excitons, which leads to an improvement in the mixed linker MOF’s electrical conductivity. The EIS observations of the mixed linker MOF significantly supports the photoluminescence measurements. Subsequently, a comparable transient photocurrent analysis (Fig. 4b) has been conducted on the UiO-66, UiO-66- ${\cdot\}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , and UML-2 MOFs in alternately dark and visible light $(420\mathrm{nm})$ illumination setting to offer insights on the photo-generated electron/hole separation efficiencies. It is usually understood that photocurrent production primarily associated with the diffusion of photo-generated electrons to the back contact and holes depleted by acceptors in electrolyte [50,51]. In Fig. 4b, the UML-2 MOF shows a rise in transient current indicating an enhanced excitons lifetime for the mixed linker material with respect to the pristine counterparts. This behavior can be associated with the effective exciton segregation or lowering recombination probability via the insertion of two different ditopic linkers in a single MOF grid, thereby leading to superior photocatalytic performance. Furthermore, the transient photocurrent analysis validates the described earlier PL and EIS outcomes.
## 3.4. TCH photo-degradation studies
Typically, a prevailing antibiotic TCH was employed as the model pollutant, to assess the efficacy of the prepared photocatalysts. Apparently, TCH remediation occurs through two steps process involving physisorption on photocatalyst surface followed by photo-degradation. In order to achieve the TCH adsorption–desorption equilibrium, a solution containing $0.02\ \mathrm{gm}$ catalyst in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of 20 ppm TCH suspension was thoroughly stirred under dark conditions for $^\mathrm{~1~h~}$ using the prepared materials; UiO-66, UiO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ along with mixed linker MOFs UML-1, 2, 3. Besides, experiments exhibited no photo degradation output in the absence catalyst and sun light irradiation. However for stiring in dark the prepared materials showed some removal of TCH which might be due to the adsorption of TCH molecules on the MOF surface as shown in Fig. 5a. Additionally, Fig. 5a implies that, the UML-2 sample showed maximum degradation efficiency in comparison to UML-1, UML-3, UiO- ${\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and UiO-66 by solar light exposure. Post-irradiation for a period of 1 hr, UML-2 showed $91.8\%$ TCH degradation whereas in the same time period pristine MOFs; UiO-66 and UiO- $66\substack{-\mathsf{N H}_{2}}$ displayed $29.4\%$ and $47.6\%$ degradation efficacy respectively. High photo-catalytic output of mixed linker
MOF can be attributed to following factors: (a) Suitable light absorption capability of the mixed linker composite as compared to pristine counterpart i.e. well depicted by wide absorption edge of UV–Vis DRS analysis. (b) Superior charge separation that is clearly seen through TRPL, EIS measurement, Transient and PL studies. Subsequently, a more detailed investigation of the TCH photo-degradation was explored through the following experiments: (1) Reaction kinetics (2) pH variation (3) TCH concentration and catalyst dosage (4) effect of coexisting ions (5) Quenching analysis and (6) Catalyst stability test.
The rate of photo degradation was assessed by examining the TCH degradation rates in a regular interval. TCH photodegradation followed first-order reaction kinetics (Eq. (6)) as depicted in Fig. 5b. Furthermore Fig. 5c represents, the logarithm form (Eq. (7)) of the preceding Eq. (6) and (7), is as follows:
$$
\Gamma=\frac{-d c}{d t}=\mathbf{k}\prime\mathbf{c}
$$
$$
\ln{(\frac{C}{C_{0}})}=\mathrm{k}\prime\mathrm{t}
$$
where r: rate of reaction, $\mathbf{k}^{\prime}$ : first order rate constant, t: time, C: TCH concentration at time t, ${{C}_{0}}$ : initial TCH concentration.
It is evident from both kinetics graphs that UML-2 seems to have the optimum photocatalytic output towards TCH photodegradation. The experimental data linearly fits the ln $\begin{array}{r l}{\lefteqn{\big(\frac{C}{C_{0}}\big)}}\end{array}$ vs time graph with a negative slope, that demonstrates a pseudo first order kinetic for TCH degradation reaction. Kinetics studies were performed to evaluate the optimal period for TCH photodegradation. The rate constants obtained are tabularized in Table S3. More over the first order rate constant for UML-2 is 0.032 that is much higher than the parents MOFs suggesting faster degradation kinetics in the mixed linker MOF. Typically, the degradation was monitored at uniform time periods (0, 10, 20, 30, 40, 50, $60~\mathrm{{min}};$ ) by using $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of 20 ppm TCH solution which displayed degradation output of $27.4\ \%$ (in dark), $33.2\ \%$ , $42.3\ \%$ , $55\ \%$ 68.2, $78.3\%$ and $91.8\ \%$ respectively for UML-2.
Moreover, the role of solution $\mathsf{p H}$ remains important as it affects both the TCH speciation and catalyst surface charge. It is well know that in aqueous environment, TCH acts as an amphiphilic species that occurs as cationic form $\mathrm{(pH}<3.3\mathrm{)}$ , Zwitterion (3.3 $\angle\mathsf{p H}<7.7\cdot$ and anionic form $\mathrm{\langlepH>7.7\rangle}$ [52]. Moreover, UiO-66 and UiO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ tend to have $\mathrm{\pH_{\mathrm{pzc}}}$ (pzc: point of zero charge) values of 6 and 4.6 respectively [53,54]. Hence at lower pH $\scriptstyle(=3)$ both TCH and MOF surface face repulsion from each other as both of them have positive charges on them. Similarly, at higher pH the anionic TCH and negatively charged MOF surface makes it difficult to facilitate superior TCH degradation. Yet at $\mathsf{p H}=5$ the TCH zwitterion is easily attracted to the negatively charged MOF surface leading to optimal TCH degradation as shown in Fig. S9 [55,56]. Additionally, the optimal catalyst dosage for TCH removal by UML-2 is represented in Fig. S10a. Typically, at lower dosage (10 and $15{\mathrm{~mg}}{\cdot}$ ) the TCH removal was low due the unavailability of active sites. However, upon increasing the UML-2 dosage $20~\mathrm{mg}$ there was significant increase in TCH degradation but upon further dosages increase the degradation was not affected significantly hence making $20~\mathrm{mg}$ of UMl-2 in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ solution at a constant TCH concentration to be the optimal dosage [56]. Similarly, in Fig. S10b the optimal initial TCH concentration using UML-2 is evaluated. It’s clearly seen that upon increasing the TCH concentration the removal efficiencies significantly decrease. This can be attributed to the coverage of UML-2 active sites with TCH molecules leading to the decrease in photo-degradation output. However, 20 ppm TCH remains the optimal concentration for degradation by UML-2 [57]. Additionally it is very much necessary to study the impact of some commonly occurring anionic $(\mathsf{S O}_{4}^{2-}$ $\mathsf{N O}_{3}^{-}$ , $C0_{3}^{2-}$ , Cl-) and cationic $(\mathsf{N a}^{+},\mathsf{K}^{+},\mathsf{F e}^{2+},\mathsf{F e}^{3+})$ that acts as coexisting ions in aqueous media on TCH degradation by UML-2 (Fig. S10c). Typically the presence of anions lead to slightly decrease in the degradation of TCH $(\mathsf{S O}_{4}^{2-}{\mathrm{>}}\mathsf{C O}_{3}^{2-}{\mathrm{>}}\mathsf{N O}_{3}^{-}{\mathrm{>}}\mathsf{C l}^{-})$ by UML2 which may be attributed to the scavenging tendency of coexisting anionic species on the photo-generated radicals [58]. Moreover, the presence of monovalent cations like $\mathsf{N a}^{+}$ and $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ do not have any significant impact on TCH degradation. But the presence of $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ or $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ in aqueous media tend to reduce the degradation as they are known to hinder the degradation process by complex formation with the antibiotic molecules as well as filtering out light which is necessary for the photoreaction [59].
Determining the role of active species (OH., $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{h}}^{+}$ , $0_{2}\cdot^{-}$ and e-) in a given photocatalytic redox reaction remains a pivotal factor in contemplating the materials performance [60–62]. A series of trapping tests were performed to ascertain the active species involved in TCH degradation by UML-2. Typically by introducing desired scavengers such as; Citric acid/CA $\mathrm{\cdoth^{\ast}}$ scavenger), isopropyl alcohol/IPA (OH scavenger), P-benzoquinone/PBQ $\cdot\mathrm{O}_{2}\cdot\overline{{\mathbf{\Omega}}}$ scavenger), dimethyl sulfoxide/DMSO $\cdot\mathtt{e}^{-}$ scavenger) to the reaction as shown in Fig. S11 were used to entrap the active species produced [63]. From the scavenger bar graph its evident that the TCH photodegradation rate significantly declined to $20.6\%$ in presence of PBQ or ${\boldsymbol{0}}_{2}^{-}$ scavenger with respect to the pristine reaction condition. Likewise, the photo-degradation rates changed to $37.8\%$ , $51.2\%$ , and $67.1\%$ upon the addition of $\mathrm{{IPA}/\mathrm{{OH}\cdot}}$ scavenger, $\mathsf{D M S O}/$ $\mathbf{e}^{-}$ scavenger and $\mathsf{C A/h^{+}}$ scavenger respectively. It is conclusive that the influence of the active species on TCH photo-degradation are in the sequence; $0_{2}\cdot^{-}{>}\mathsf{O H}^{.}$ . Moreover, LC-MS analysis was carried out as shown in Fig. S12 to ascertain the intermediates formed during the photo-degradation of TCH. Mixed linker MOF’s photo-stability as well as recyclability were assessed by taking the post photocatalysis UML-2 powders and reusing them for three successive photo-catalytic reactions cycles under the similar condition. As shown in Fig. 5d the mixed linker MOF displayed satisfactory stability and unhindered photo-catalytic performance during these reaction cycles. Diffraction patterns of the as synthesized UML-2 and used one did not show any appreciable changes (Fig. S13) which implies that the MOF grid remains intact even after three consecutive cycles. Furthermore, the UML-2 MOF was stirred in water for $24\mathrm{h}$ to contemplate its aqueous stability; it did not show any significant changes in its diffraction pattern thus suggesting the mixed linker MOF to be very stable in aqueous environment. The robustness of mixed linker MOF; UML-2 is inherited from the zirconium-carboxylate MOFs. Typically, ${\cal Z}\mathrm{r}^{4+}$ ions acts as the hard acid which has high polarization towards the hard basic type carboxylate moieties of the terephthalate linkers which results in ultra-stable $Z\mathrm{r}{-}0$ bonding leading to creation of the stable metal organic frameworks [64,65]. Hence, the mixed linker MOF is an aqueous stable and robust material for the photo-degradation of antibiotic based pollutant entities, which is critical for practical usage.
## 3.5. Photocatalytic hydrogen generation
Photocatalytic output of the solvothermally fabricated photocatalysts; UiO-66, $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ and UML-1, 2, 3 were evaluated thoroughly towards hydrogen evolution reaction. Hydrogen production reaction was executed in a closed batch type quartz reactor with $0.1\mathrm{~L~}$ holding capacity containing $20{\mathrm{~mg}}$ of prepared catalyst sample in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of the MeOH-Water $(10\%\lor/\lor)$ solution under ambient conditions. Prior to irradiation, the contents of the batch reactor were vigorously mixed along with nitrogen purging $(30\mathrm{\min})$ for the expulsion of any dissolved gases. A Xe-lamp (300 W) was utilized as the light source for a period of $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ towards hydrogen evolution. Moreover, the evolved gases were analyzed by using gas chromatography (GC-7890B/Agilent Technology/molecular sieve-5 Å) accompanied with a thermal conductivity detector (TCD). Initially blank analysis readings were taken in the absence lamp or catalyst in order to confirm that the evolved hydrogen gas is solely derived from methanol-water solution in the presence of MOF based photo-catalyst and light only. Fig. 6a illustrates the results acquired for photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production using the prepared MOFs. It is undeniable that the pristine MOFs; UiO-66 and UiO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ showed feeble hydrogen evolution performance i.e. 15 and $115\ \upmu\mathrm{mole}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}$ respectively resulting from their low light absorption, wide band gap (as in UiO-66) and fast exciton recombination. However, the mixed linker functionalization satergy significantly improved the hydrogen evolution rates, which is a resultant of superior charge carrier separation and reduced charge transfer resistance. Additionally, the band edge structure of parent MOFs is well inherited by the mixed linker MOFs, whose LUMO is amply negative to promote hydrogen production by visible light illumination. Amidst, the three mixed linker composites, UML-2 showed superior photo-performance of $247.88\ \upmu\mathrm{mole}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}$ than that of both the parent MOFs as well as other mixed linker MOFs that is consistent with the PL outcomes. Hydrogen production capacity of UML-2 along with several other photocatalysts is comparatively presented in Table S4. Furthermore, catalytic durability of the mixed linker MOF, UML-2 was demonstrated until four consecutive hydrogen production cycles (for 1 h each) with unhindered performance as illustrated in Fig. 6b. Moreover, the apparent conversion efficiency (ACE) of UML-2 was evaluated to be $1.9\%$ by using the expression as represented in Eq. (8):
