## 3.6. Mechanistic insights
Underlying mechanistic pathways of the one pot fabricated mixed linker MOFs towards TCH photo-degradation and $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ production is represented in Scheme 3, which is thoroughly described based on the above-mentioned analytical outcomes. In fact, photocatalytic activity is carried-out through two step process, i.e. (i) physisorption of the reactant species on the catalyst surface (ii) Photocatalytic reaction of the adsorbed reactant with the photoactive catalyst. For this purpose, a photocatalyst has to meet two specific physio-chemical features, i.e. it should have high active surface area to adsorb the reactant species and secondly it should have excellent charge carrier stability to carry out the reaction. In the present study the bi-linker MOF i.e. UML series full fills both the criteria in order to show improved photocatalytic outcome in comparison to their pristine counterpart.
Mostly suitable bandgap, delayed charge recombination and active surface area are three most crucial physico-chemical parameter that affects the activity of a photocatalyst. In the present study both the linkers i.e. BDC and BDC- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ has been used for the preparation of the bilinker MOF i.e. UML to tune the bandgap of UiO-66 and check the morphological as well as surface activity of UiO-66- $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ . The bandgap was calculated by considering the UV–Vis DRS analysis and was found to be $2.7~\mathrm{eV}$ which proves blue shift in bandgap of pristine UiO-66 (3.6 eV) and shows a slight variation than the bandgap of $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ $(2.67~\mathrm{eV})$ . Additionally, MottSchottky analysis was carried out to get the Flat band potential and hence the corresponding Band edge potentials i.e. 2.17 and $-0.53\ \mathrm{eV}$ for HOMO and LUMO respectively at $\mathsf{p H}7$ . The corresponding band edge potential are suitable towards the hydroxide ion oxidation and superoxide reduction at corresponding HOMO and LUMO to further carryout the antibiotic degradation and $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ evolution [48,66]. The basic mechanistic activity of the UML material is enhanced because of the improved light absorption capacity due to the synergistic effect of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -cluster and BDC- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ linker. The
Metal-linker combination and diverse linker implication enhances the ligand to metal charge transfer (LMCT) in the MOF structure. Moreover, from the TRPL analysis it is seen that the mixed linker MOF (UML-2) has higher lifetime of the photo-generated electrons in comparison with the parent MOFs (UiO-66 and UiO-66- ${\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ ) this trend is well reflected in its photocatalytic performance. Moreover the PL, EIS and transient photocurrent studies further support these claims thus making UML-2 a superior photocatalyst with respect to the individual parent MOFs. In addition to it, XPS analysis spectrum analysis confirms the presence of oxygen vacancy (OVs) in the synthesized UML material. It can be predicted that, the diversification in linker property might be responsible for it, as oxygen vacancy is rarely observed in its pristine counterpart. The presence of oxygen vacancy additionally support to enhance the charge carrier stability and hence improves the photocatalytic activity [43,67]. The mixed linker Zr-MOF upon light illumination, was excited to produce photo-generated excitons and lead to the transfer of electrons from HOMO to LUMO. Along with the efficacious reduction in the recombination probability due to the presence of dual linkers and OVs in the framework. Moreover, these photo-generated excitons are assembled at HOMO and LUMO of the corresponding UML-2 MOF based photocatalyst that acts as a key tool for the generation of active species such as; OH. and $\boldsymbol{0}_{2}^{-}$ radicals respectively which were then detected vis NBT and TA tests as represented in Fig. S6. Additionally, the superoxide radicals $(\boldsymbol{0}_{2}^{\cdot^{-}})$ acts as the leading active species while OH. contributed barely in TCH photo-degradation under visible light illumination this explanation is also supported by the scavenger tests as shown in Fig. S6. These experimental results suggest that the superoxide radical performs the pivotal role in the photocatalytic TCH degradation reaction. Moreover, LC-MS analysis was carried out to contemplate the pathways of TCH photo-degradation and intermediates involved in the reaction. TCH degradation followed deamination, demethylation, dehydroxylation and ring open reaction paths to produce various intermediates as shown subsequently. Intermediates produced were detected through the molecular ion peaks with different $m/z$ values LC-MS technique which has been depicted in Fig. S7. Typically, the TCH photodegradation was driven by the attack of superoxide radicals form which a sum sixteen intermediates were identified. TCH photodegradation was projected according to the intermediates identified that is depicted in Scheme 2. The present study represents four modes for the formation of sixteen intermediates at $m/z$ values; 433, 388, 363, 346, 303, 280, 223, 207, 193, 164, 154, 145, 122, 106, 74 and 60 via partial elimination, reduction, oxidation and ring opening reactions in which the fragmented intermediates were very much correlated with the reported works [19,23,68]. The peaks at 106, 74 and 60 further underwent mineralization to produce non-toxic smaller inorganic molecules $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},\mathrm{CO}_{2}\right)$ and $\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}$ ).
Additionally, the one pot fabricated mixed linker composite UML-2 was applied for water photo-reduction reaction to generate hydrogen gas in the existence a hole scavenger; $10\%$ MeOH/deionised water $(\mathsf{V}/\mathsf{V})$ solution. The Presence of $\boldsymbol{-}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{N}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}_{2}$ functional groups in the mixed linker framework of UML-2 promoted the hydrophilic nature of the framework that is similar to UiO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ MOF and it facilitated the easy $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ molecules adsorption on the Zr-MOF based photo-catalyst surface.[40] Subsequently, the protons produced from dissociated water molecules takes up photo-generated e-s from LUMO of UML-2, and undergo reduction to produce $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ gas.
## 4. Conclusion
Summarily, robust and water stable MOF based nanomaterial has been designed and applied towards photocatalytic hydrogen/ $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production and antibiotic (Tetracycline hydrochloride/TCH)
degradation. The following are the salient features of the fabricated nanomaterial:
(i) Single component photocatalyst was designed via one pot solvothermal approach to design a mixed linker zirconium based metal organic framework. The one pot mixed linker strategy remains an advantageous route for preparation of visible light active isostructural frameworks that can mitigate the need for the complex methods of MOF modification involving post synthetic modification or composite formation with other semiconductor based nanomaterials.
(ii) Through the mixed linker strategy the introduction second linker (amino-terepthalic acid) significantly narrowed the band gap (3.68–2.68 eV) and made the band structure of UiO-66 more suitable for photocatalytic applications. Moreover the along with improved visible light absorption, exciton separation and oxygen vacancies in the framework lead to better performances than both the parent MOFs. Moreover, the life time of photo-generated electrons is enhance in the mixed linker framework as seen from TRPL analysis. Cumulatively, these features enhanced the optical and electrochemical properties that eventually aided in the superior photocatalytic activity of the aqueous stable mixed linker MOFs.
(iii) The synthesized UML-2 showed superior photocatalytic $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ production rate of $247.88\ \upmu\mathrm{mole\h^{-}}$ accompanied by an ACE value of $1.9\%$ which is twice higher than the parent $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ MOF and several folds better than parent UiO66. Additionally, the TCH photo-degradation by UML-2 MOF showed an enhanced efficacy of $91.8\%$ , which followed pseudo first order type kinetics model along with the rate constant value of 0.032 suggesting faster degradation than the parent frameworks. It is noteworthy that the reported hydrogen evolution rates by Zr-MOF based nanomaterials such as; UiO-66/CdS $(153.2\ \mathrm{\textmumol}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1};$ , UiO-66 $\mathrm{\slashg-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ $(14.11\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}.$ ), $\mathrm{g{-}C_{3}N_{4}/U i O{-}66{-}N H_{2}}$ ( $152.2~\upmu\mathrm{mol}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ ), $g_{-}$ $\mathrm{C_{3}N_{4}/U i O{-}66/N i_{2}P}$ $(200~\upmu\mathrm{mol}$ in $5\ \mathrm{~h~}$ ) and TCH photodegradation such as; AgI/BiOI/UiO-66 $(90.1\%)$ , $\mathsf{S m V O_{4}/U i O-}$ $66\substack{-\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}}$ $(50\%)$ , $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@\mathrm{UiO}-66\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ $(75\%)$ represents the superiority of the present work (TCH degradation $91.8\%$ and $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ evolution rate $247.88\ \upmu\mathrm{mole}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}$ by UML-2) [24–26,29–32].   
(iv) Moreover, for the mixed linker MOF (UML-2) the active species responsible for the photo-degradation of the TCH antibiotic follows the sequence; hydroxyl $\left(\mathrm{OH^{\cdot}}\right)\ <$ superoxide radicals $(\boldsymbol{0}_{2}^{\cdot\bar{\cdot}})$ as the band structure of the mixed linker MOF is suitable for the formation of both of these radicals.   
(v) The mixed linker MOF remains stable under aqueous and photocatalytic reaction conditions as conferred from its post reaction XRD analysis. As a result, the mixed linker MOF could be promptly employed as a potent MOF based photocatalyst with superior antibiotic degradations as well as hydrogen generation capabilities.
